WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.114
Parameter count describes one dimension of model size,

00:00:03.114 --> 00:00:05.374
but architecture and implementation also matter.

00:00:05.374 --> 00:00:08.387
More parameters usually require more memory and compute,

00:00:08.387 --> 00:00:13.284
yet a larger model is not automatically better for every task or runtime.

00:00:13.284 --> 00:00:18.558
Quantization reduces numerical precision to lower memory use and often improve practical inference speed.

00:00:18.558 --> 00:00:22.702
Lower precision can affect output quality differently across models and tasks.

00:00:22.702 --> 00:00:27.222
Test the actual quantization rather than assuming one label guarantees a result.

00:00:27.222 --> 00:00:31.742
G G U F is a common model-file format used by llama.

00:00:31.742 --> 00:00:32.496
cpp-compatible runtimes.

00:00:32.496 --> 00:00:35.509
A file name may describe architecture and quantization,

00:00:35.509 --> 00:00:39.276
but compatibility also depends on runtime support, tokenizer, prompt template,

00:00:39.276 --> 00:00:40.406
and model metadata.

00:00:40.406 --> 00:00:44.927
The context window is the number of tokens the system can consider

00:00:44.927 --> 00:00:46.810
in a request and conversation.

00:00:46.810 --> 00:00:49.447
Longer context increases memory use, prompt-processing time,

00:00:49.447 --> 00:00:51.707
and the chance of irrelevant information.

00:00:51.707 --> 00:00:55.098
Advertised maximum context is not a default operating target.

00:00:55.098 --> 00:00:58.865
During generation, the key-value cache stores attention information for context.

00:00:58.865 --> 00:01:03.008
Cache size grows with model design, context, concurrency, precision, and runtime.

00:01:03.008 --> 00:01:09.035
A model that fits at startup may run out of memory during longer or simultaneous requests.

00:01:09.035 --> 00:01:13.179
Fit planning includes weights, cache, runtime overhead, display use, operating-system load,

00:01:13.179 --> 00:01:14.309
and safety margin.

00:01:14.309 --> 00:01:18.076
When the whole model does not fit on the accelerator,

00:01:18.076 --> 00:01:23.726
partial GPU offload can place some work on the GPU and some on the CPU.

00:01:23.726 --> 00:01:26.740
Measure speed and stability under the intended workload.
